  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the dining room, an angry guest complains to the pastry chef Louis about finding something hard in his dessert. Louis is terrified. Leva bravely steps in, claims to be the head chef, and takes responsibility. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что это у меня в десерте?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я из-за вас чуть зуб не сломал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Простите нас, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ты сначала по-русски говорить научись!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Зачем ты сюда приехал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Чтобы люди ломали зубы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я кондитер, это моя вина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Оставьте кондитера в покое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А ты кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я шеф-повар этого ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я отвечаю за всё на кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас есть претензии ко мне?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вот моя претензия, держите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вкусный десерт, большое спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что это у меня в десерте?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is this in my dessert?' Notice how 'what' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'dessert'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я из-за вас чуть зуб не сломал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I almost broke a tooth because of you!' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'broke'. Then Louis responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Простите нас, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Please forgive us.' Louis uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'excuse me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ты сначала по-русски говорить научись!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'First, learn to speak Russian!' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'learn' turns the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Зачем ты сюда приехал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why did you come here?' Notice how 'what for' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'came'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Чтобы люди ломали зубы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So people would break their teeth?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'so that' with 'tooth'. Then Louis responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я кондитер, это моя вина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am the pastry chef, it is my fault.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'of wine'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Оставьте кондитера в покое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Leave the pastry chef alone.' Lyova uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'leave'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А ты кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And who are you?' Notice how 'and' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я шеф-повар этого ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am the head chef of this restaurant.' Lyova links 'I' directly with 'head chef' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я отвечаю за всё на кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am responsible for everything in the kitchen.' Lyova links 'I' directly with 'to answer' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас есть претензии ко мне?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have complaints against me?' Notice how 'at' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вот моя претензия, держите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is my complaint, hold it.' the guest uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'hold'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вкусный десерт, большое спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Delicious dessert, thank you very much.' Louis combines 'delicious' with 'thank you' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
